   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">06 Dec 2023 | News Release: https://www.adb.org/news/adb-support-small-and-medium-sized-enterprises-uzbekistan</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">09 Dec 2021 | News Release: https://www.adb.org/news/100-million-adb-loan-promote-small-and-medium-enterprises-development-uzbekistan</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Loan 4156: 100% of contracts awarded and 100% disbursed as of 11 Apr 2025</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Loan 4157: 100% of contracts awarded and 100% disbursed as of 11 Apr 2025</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TA 6865: 17% disbursed as of 11 Apr 2025</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tenders and Contracts Awarded: https://www.adb.org/projects/42007-020/main#project-tenders</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">a. To ensure effective and efficient governance of the public credit guarantee (PCG) scheme for SMEs: (i) Ministry of Economic Development and Poverty Reduction (MOEDPR), in accordance with Order No. 35 aligned the State Fund for Support of Entrepreneurial Activity (State Fund) with the World Bank&#x2019;s 16 principles for Partial Credit Guarantees (PCG) by; a) operationalizing separate and more transparent accounting systems for the guarantee and interest compensation windows, b) operationalizing a transparent operational framework for the issuance and management of guarantees with clear eligibility and qualification criteria, and introducing climate change risk management considerations into the environment and social management system (ESMS) used for project screening, c) operationalizing an effective risk management framework with a risk-based pricing policy and transparent claims management process, and d) operationalizing a Management Information System with quarterly reporting, external audit and variance analysis against budgets; and (ii) Presidential Decree No. 6263 established an independent board of directors for the PCG which is now functioning.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">b. To ensure that the State Fund expands PCGs to women&#x2019;s business, (i) Ministry of Finance (MOF) approved the allocation of SUM200 billion to the State Fund in the 2021 state budget, and further funds will be allocated on an annual basis on the demand of the State Fund to achieve issuance of at least 25% of new guarantees to women&#x2019;s businesses, and (ii) government approved a microenterprise- focused PCG product (with a higher guarantee ratio of 70%&#x2013;75% and a low maximum loan amount cap of $20,000).</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">c. To expand access to finance by enabling the use moveable collateral, including accounts receivables and warehousing receipt financing, as security in financial contracts: (i) CBU abolished CBU Regulation 953 &quot;On Procedures for conducting factoring operations by commercial banks on the territory of Uzbekistan,&quot; and (ii) CBU abolished in August 2021, the requirements (approved in 2000 under CBU Regulation No. 905) that constrained the effective used of moveable collateral in SME credit financing by commercial banks.</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
   <indicator measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">d. To: (i) promote financial innovations (FinTech, crowdfunding, etc.) to support SME financing, (ii) promote non-collateral-based lending to SMEs by expanding credit-history information, and (iii) support development of the nonbank finance sector&#x2019;s ability to serve SMEs, CBU submitted for COM approval the law on NonBank Credit Institutions and Microfinance Activities comprising: (i) legislative amendments allowing the creation of a regulatory sandbox, (ii) obligating leasing, and selected retail companies to provide credit-history information to the credit bureau, and (iii) defining the scope of services and governance principles of factoring organizations, microfinance institutions, and pawnshops and the corresponding regulatory role of the Central Bank of Uzbekistan (CBU).</narrative>
    </title>
   </indicator>
  </result>
